+no stick zoom, so zoom stays on the keyboard and mouse.
+
+> **This build's pad defaults differ from upstream's.** Upstream puts movement on
+> the triggers and the left stick and the four item shortcuts on the D-pad, which
+> a stickless handheld cannot steer with at all. Here movement is on the D-pad
+> (stick as a second binding), and the freed triggers carry the two actions that
+> were otherwise stick-only: **L2** the 90-degree camera snap (`I_CAMERA`) and
+> **R2** the dodge (`I_ESQUIVE`, stock: the stick clicks). The item shortcuts are
+> unbound; those items stay reachable from the inventory. `GamepadKeysDefault` in
+> [SOURCES/INPUT.CPP](../SOURCES/INPUT.CPP) carries this layout, so the gamepad
+> config screen's "restore defaults" returns here rather than to a layout the
+> device cannot steer back out of.
+
+## Camera-relative movement (community; stickless handhelds)
+
+Stock hero control is "tank": `I_LEFT` / `I_RIGHT` rotate `Beta` on the spot and
+`I_UP` walks along it. On a handheld with no analog stick there is no free-look
+axis at all, so the hero's facing is the only thing that can steer — and tank
+turning is what most players bounce off.
+
+With `CameraRelativeMove` on (cfg key, cvar `move_relative`, menu
+Options → Controls; **on by default in this fork**, which differs from the
+engine's own opt-in default — see below), the 8-way directional input instead
+names a direction in the **camera's** frame.
+
+It is **not D-pad specific**, despite where it came from. It reads the
+`I_UP`/`I_DOWN`/`I_LEFT`/`I_RIGHT` bits, so every directional source gets it
+alike: the D-pad, the left stick (`ApplyStickDirection` in
+[JOYSTICK.CPP](../SOURCES/JOYSTICK.CPP) digitises the stick to the same eight
+directions before it reaches those bits, so a stick gets 8-way too, with no
+analog magnitude) and the keyboard arrows. The hero auto-turns to face that world
+direction and walks forward on the ordinary `GEN_ANIM_MARCHE`. There are no
+strafe frames in the animation set — only `GEN_ANIM_MARCHE` / `RECULE` /
+`GAUCHE` / `DROITE` plus the one-shot `GEN_ANIM_ESQUIVE_*` dodges — so
+turn-then-walk is the only way to reach an arbitrary heading. *Down*
+therefore turns the hero around and walks, rather than playing the backpedal.
+
+### Two reference frames
+
+`CamRelCameraYaw` supplies "away from the camera", and the two cases are not
+alike:
+
+- **Interior (isometric): a constant.** `LongProjectPointIso`
+  ([LIB386/3D/LPROJISO.CPP](../LIB386/3D/LPROJISO.CPP)) carries no camera-angle
+  term — screen X comes from `(x - z)`, screen Y from `(x + z)` — and the
+  interior camera never rotates. Screen-up is therefore the fixed world heading
+  `ISO_SCREEN_UP_BETA` (2560) and screen-right is 1536, exactly 1024 apart,
+  matching the 8-way offsets. No camera state is read and the latch is a no-op.
+  This is ordinary isometric 8-way control.
+- **Exterior: `(2048 - BetaCam)`.** Under the classic cameras that is a stable
+  per-scene angle. Under Auto camera it tracks the hero, which is the case the
+  latch below exists for.
+
+**It does not require Auto camera, and must not turn it on.** An earlier cut had
+the menu toggle enable `FollowCamera` alongside it, on the reasoning that the
+scheme needed a tracked camera yaw. It does not — and Auto camera's per-frame
+terrain refresh costs about 15 ms/frame on handheld hardware (measured on an
+Amlogic S922X at 848x480: 52 frames/s with it on, 235 with it off), so that
+coupling silently halved the frame rate as the price of changing control scheme.
+
+### The latch (why the reference yaw is frozen)
+
+The reference yaw is **latched when the 8-way direction changes**, not read every
+frame. This is the whole design, not an optimisation.
+
+Auto camera's yaw target is `(2048 - heroBeta)` ([PERSO.CPP](../SOURCES/PERSO.CPP),
+`UpdateFollowCameraExt`): the camera tracks the hero with loop gain 1, so it has
+no yaw of its own to be relative *to*. A live camera-relative mapping against it
+feeds back on itself — the hero turns, the camera follows, the mapping shifts,
+and holding one direction walks a circle. Latching freezes the frame for the
+duration of a press, which kills the feedback and has the nicer property that the
+mapping always matches what was on screen at the instant the player pressed.
+
+The latch is re-taken when the 8-way direction changes, when the scheme was not
+driving on the previous frame (so a scene load or a cutscene re-syncs it), and on
+Center camera (`CameraRelativeReset`, called from the `I_RETURN` handler), which
+swings the camera under the player.
+
+### Scope
+
+Active for free player control (`MOVE_MANUAL`) in **both** exterior and
+isometric interior scenes, and under **any** camera. Life-script-driven movement
+is untouched. It stands down while an aiming or attack input is held
+(`I_ESQUIVE`, `I_THROW`, `I_ACTION_M`) and for a jump in flight
+(`Jumping & 1023`), because those own the hero's facing or body.
+Dodges are one-shot moves keyed to it, and while throw or a combat action is held
+the stock code steers by adding `GetDeltaMove(&BoundAngle.Move)` straight onto
+`Beta` (*"control direction pendant visée"*) — the same accumulator this scheme's
+auto-turn drives. Leaving it active means both drive one accumulator, and the
+hero snaps toward the latched heading then crawls instead of aiming smoothly.
+Suppressing only the *turn* is not enough: the stock aim-steer needs the
+accumulator speed `ManualRealAngle` sets from the real left/right, which the
+remap clears. Standing the whole scheme down hands aiming back to the stock
+tank-style steer those actions were designed around.
+
+A jump in flight is excluded for a third reason again. Stock protects an
+in-flight jump with an early exit from the `MOVE_MANUAL` case, guarded on
+`LastMyJoy & (I_UP | I_DOWN)` — the previous frame's **real** pad. The remap
+makes the current frame synthetic while `LastMyJoy` stays real, so the guard
+misses, execution falls through to the direction block, and its unconditional
+`InitAnim(GEN_ANIM_MARCHE, ANIM_REPEAT)` overrides the `ANIM_THEN` jump — the
+hero never leaves the ground. Standing down for the whole jump keeps stock's own
+protection intact rather than trying to reproduce it. That early exit is also the
+only path out of the case that skips the `Input` restore, so it undoes the remap
+itself.
+
+**Two gates, not one.** The input remap and the auto-turn are suppressed
+separately, and conflating them is a bug:
+
+- **Ladders** suppress the *whole scheme*, and on `PtrZoneClimb` (the zone) rather
+  than on `FlagClimbing` (the state). A ladder needs literal up *and* down, and
+  the remap only ever emits up, so there would be no way back down. Keying on the
+  zone means the scheme stands down on approach instead of mid-climb.
+- **Pushing a block, a jump in flight, an anim that owns rotation
+  (`ANIM_MASTER_ROT`), and a held behaviour action** suppress only the
+  *auto-turn* (`canturn` at the call site). The remap must keep feeding "forward"
+  through them: the player entered those states by pressing a camera-relative
+  direction and their finger is still on it. Deactivating the whole scheme on
+  `Pushing` strands the push the instant it starts — the stock block then sees
+  the real (say) "left", never reaches its `if (Pushing)` branch, and rotates out
+  of contact. When turning is suppressed the accumulator is parked with
+  `RestartMove`, exactly as `ManualRealAngle` does, so no delta banks up and
+  dumps into `Beta` when control returns.
+
+Implementation: `CamRelActive` / `CamRelDirection` / `CamRelTurn` in
+[SOURCES/OBJECT.CPP](../SOURCES/OBJECT.CPP). It is resolved at the **top** of the
+`MOVE_MANUAL` case, before the `Comportement` cases, and that ordering is
+load-bearing: the jetpack / Proto-Pack hover forces `I_UP` further down, which
+must not read as a player direction (it would latch and auto-turn the hero on no
+input at all), and the "can't start the Proto-Pack in water" guard clears `I_UP`,
+which must still win over the remap.
+
+The scheme rewrites `Input` to a plain "forward", so walking, pushing, the anim
+and the `LastJoyFlag` bookkeeping all run exactly as they do on the tank path,
+then restores it before `LastInput` / `LastMyJoy` are latched so edge detection
+still sees the real pad. Turning rides the bound-angle `MOVE` accumulator
+(`ChangeSpeedBoundAngleMove` / `GetBoundAngleMove`) — shortest arc, clamps on
+arrival, carries its sub-unit remainder, so it is frame-rate independent (see
+`README.md` in the port's source package) unlike the animation-baked
+translation it steers.
+
+| Key | Default | Description |
+|-----|---------|-------------|
+| `CameraRelativeMove` | 1 | enable (0 = classic tank controls). Upstream's equivalent would be 0; this fork targets stickless handhelds, where tank control is the thing players bounce off |
+| `CameraRelativeTurnSpeed` | 2560 | auto-turn rate, Beta units/sec (4096 = full circle; stock manual turn is 1024) |
 
